.TH LINUXHARDWAREMONITOR 1 "October 2026" "linux-hardware-monitor 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
LinuxHardwareMonitor \- sensors, performance graphs and a task manager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B LinuxHardwareMonitor
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Linux Hardware Monitor shows the temperatures, fans, voltages, loads and
clocks of the CPU, memory, disks, network adapters, graphics cards and the
battery, together with a process manager, systemd services and startup
applications. It runs without root privileges and reads only what the kernel
and the system services expose; a value that cannot be read is shown as
N/A, never as zero.
.PP
Without options the real hardware of this computer is shown. The other
data sources are opt-in and are always announced in the window.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-help, \-h
Show the list of options and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version, \-v
Show the version, the build (the commit it was made from), the Qt version and
the license, and exit. The first line is always "LinuxHardwareMonitor <version>".
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-json
Print every sensor once as JSON and exit. No window or display is needed.
Unavailable sensors have a null value.
.TP
.BI \-\-dump\-delay " ms"
With \fB\-\-dump\-json\fR: wait this long between the two samples that rates
need (default 500).
.TP
.B \-\-compact
With \fB\-\-dump\-json\fR: print one line instead of indented output.
.TP
.BI \-\-mock " profile.json"
Simulate other hardware from a profile. Profiles for several systems are
installed in /usr/share/linux\-hardware\-monitor/mocks.
.TP
.BI \-\-mock\-speed " factor"
Run the simulated clock faster, for example 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-run\-scenario " n"
Trigger simulation action number \fIn\fR (0 is the first) right away.
.TP
.B \-\-scenario\-panel
Open the simulation scenario panel at start.
.TP
.BI \-\-hwmon\-root " dir"
Read hwmon data from a prepared directory tree instead of /sys/class/hwmon.
.TP
.BI \-\-proc\-root " dir"
Read /proc data from a prepared directory tree.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu\-root " dir"
Read the CPU topology from a prepared tree instead of
/sys/devices/system/cpu.
.TP
.BI \-\-power\-supply\-root " dir"
Read batteries from a prepared tree instead of /sys/class/power_supply.
.TP
.BI \-\-drm\-root " dir"
Read graphics cards from a prepared tree instead of /sys/class/drm.
.TP
.BI \-\-page " n"
Open page \fIn\fR (0 to 6) first: Performance, Processes, Services, Startup,
Sensors, Overview, Settings.
.TP
.BI \-\-resource " n"
Select this resource on the Performance page.
.TP
.B \-\-cores
Show the logical processors view of the CPU.
.TP
.BI \-\-load\-modules
Load the RAM module details at start. On real hardware this asks for
administrator rights.
.TP
.BI \-\-size " width height"
Window size in pixels.
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot " file.png"
Save a screenshot of the window and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-screenshot\-delay " ms"
Wait this long before the screenshot (default 3500) so the charts have
history.
.TP
.BI \-\-close\-after " ms"
Close the window after this many milliseconds.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/LinuxHardwareMonitor/LinuxHardwareMonitor.conf
Settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/LinuxHardwareMonitor/LinuxHardwareMonitor/history.bin
The last minutes of sensor history, restored at the next start.
.TP
.I ~/.config/autostart/linux\-hardware\-monitor.desktop
Created when "Start with the system" is switched on.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
The data source options are unusable, for example an invalid simulation
profile. The program never falls back to real data in that case.
